Z||000007||Will||06-18-2000||08:53 AM||||I would be extremely suspicious of anyone who claims to run the quarter in 10 @ 109. Typical trap speeds for a car in the low tens  are in the 135+ range.<BR>For comparison, a Hennessey Viper 650 R (that's 650 horsepower) runs 11 @ 130.<BR>A 2750 lb Fiero would need 550+ horsepower to equal that.||||reg||3
Z||000008||KnightRyder31||06-18-2000||11:22 AM||||I'm sorry Will, but I have to disagree with you. A Fiero With a high powererd V8 (450hp) could run 10's. I know this because I've seen it before. Although the guy had his car supercharged, it still was able to do it. It had a lot of mods(mostly shocks, lowererd, exhaust, trans, ect). I think the time was 10.76, or something. And the speed was over 130MPH.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shiner||06-18-2000||01:01 PM||||Sure, you could probably get a street driven Fiero in the tens with a smallblock in it.  The question is; How long will it last?  A juiced SB would shred a getrag in a heartbeat!  Remember, the Fiero was not designed to have a real hot V8 in the back, so I would assume it would also wind up the frame tighter than a $2 alarm clock.  Mustangs and Fbodies were designed to handle this kind of torque.  <P> And think about this; With the money it would take to get a Fiero into the tens, you could probably get a Mustang to pump out TWICE the power of the Fier, and wear bigger tires to boot.<p>[This message has been edited by Shiner (edited 06-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||hugh||06-18-2000||01:31 PM||||Any engine in the Fiero,if it is mounted transversly will try to lift the front end of the car because of the cars reaction to the engine torque applied to the wheels.The best way to mop up those mustangs is to put a 3.8 turbo buick engine in and hold on when you step on the gas.||||reg||1
